  "result": "I wrote the 30-minute lesson on O3, plus a quiz, its key and a cheat sheet. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser. I also couldn't run a word count, so I'm estimating its length from the section plan at roughly 3,500 words, which is inside the 2,700–6,750 window for 30 minutes.\n\n**Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`\n\nIt follows your style: motivation, definition, example, then a list of nuances, in short sentences with makerspace examples. The sections are:\n- The context window: what counts toward it, shown in a figure.\n- What is kept between steps: nothing. The program re-sends the whole history each call, so a run costs the sum of every call's input. A worked table shows 10,700 input tokens against a final context of 4,300.\n- What is kept between sessions: notes and records in storage, loaded per student.\n- Retrieval for long manuals: chunks, embeddings, BM25 keyword search, and when to skip retrieval.\n- A worked memory design for a band-saw question, then a faded practice task.\n\nEach section has a check question with instant feedback. A warm-up recalls the agent loop and tokens.\n\n**Other files:**\n- **Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` has five fresh items, and `keys/0002.json` holds the answers. I added misconception ids M7 (a bigger window removes the need to manage context) and M8 (retrieval teaches the model).\n- **Cheat sheet:** `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html`.\n- **Workspace notes:** `RESOURCES.md` is new, and `NOTES.md` is updated.\n\n**Sources:** The lesson is built on three pages I read: Anthropic's context-engineering post, the Claude context-window docs and the Contextual Retrieval post. The memory-tool link is the Spanish page, which I saw only in search results and didn't open. Look for the English version before you rely on its details.\n\nThe quiz is ready when you are. Ask me anything about the lesson.\n\nQuiz 0001 has two attempts that are still ungraded. Do you want me to grade them next?",
